Upstream: No, removes a debug print left in PR #47794 that the cluster-api backport carried over.
From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Clever Cloud <noreply@clever-cloud.com>
Date: Sun, 10 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mgr/dashboard: drop debug print(o, expected_type) in _crud.serialize

PR #47794 ships _crud.serialize() with a stray `print(o, expected_type)`
left from authoring. Reef upstream has since removed it; Pacific never
shipped _crud.py at all so we inherit the debug line via our backport
(0002-add-cluster-api.patch).

Inside the MGR daemon, sys.stdout's underlying fd is closed during
detachment. Threads that miss the redirect raise `OSError: [Errno 9]
Bad file descriptor` from `print` whenever the dynamic CRUDClass.list
serializes a NamedTuple — i.e. on every GET /api/cluster/user that
returns at least one row. Some CherryPy worker threads happen to have
a still-valid fd, which is why the failure looks like a flake (~50% of
GETs serve 500 with the unstructured CherryPy default body). It has
nothing to do with eventual consistency on auth ls.

Removing the print fixes the GET handler outright and lets the
upstream-style structured response reach clients.
---
 src/pybind/mgr/dashboard/controllers/_crud.py | 1 -
 1 file changed, 1 deletion(-)

--- a/src/pybind/mgr/dashboard/controllers/_crud.py
+++ b/src/pybind/mgr/dashboard/controllers/_crud.py
@@ -21,6 +21,5 @@ def isnamedtuple(o):
 def serialize(o, expected_type=None):
     # pylint: disable=R1705,W1116
-    print(o, expected_type)
     if isnamedtuple(o):
         hints = get_type_hints(o)
         return {k: serialize(v, hints[k]) for k, v in zip(o._fields, o)}
--
